{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/64147-the-children-s-pantheon-deities-based-on-nursery-rhymes-and-fairy-tales","id":64147,"title":"The Children's Pantheon (Deities based on Nursery Rhymes and Fairy Tales)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-02-03T11:19:16-08:00","id":64147,"title":"The Children's Pantheon (Deities based on Nursery Rhymes and Fairy Tales)","content":"From what I read in Chapter Four of Scion: God, the first Gods and Goddesses were titanspawn that were Fatebound into roles imagined by Humanity. After playing the game: Cursery The Crooked Man and Crooked Cat, I wondered children can pull off the same thing en masse. I was thinking of a pantheon that was formed by the Fatebinding belief of children, especially children of the Gods where, even though the Visitation hasn't happened, their Potential bolstered the formation of this pantheon, along with the other children who enjoyed the stories and believed them. The pantheon would have forms like Mother Goose and the Crooked Man, Little Bo Beep and Mary, and others. Throughout the years, the fairy tales of the Brothers Grimm, would be accounts of Scions of these Nursery characters, rising to godhood as their stories become legend. Therefore we get Cinderella, Snow White, Red Riding Hood, Jack, and others as well. The power of this pantheon is always in flux with the waxing and waning of children's beliefs in the stories. As for relations with the other pantheons, they get some interest by the more whimsical of each pantheon, but most ignore the Children's Pantheon for being simply &quot;childish&quot;.<br /> <br /> What do you think? Would a pantheon like this be feasible? Are there suggestions as to how to tweak it just right? <br /> <br /> Thanks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/891-jackalopevegas","name":"JackalopeVegas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-03T11:50:09-08:00","id":64168,"title":"","content":"It sounds doable.<br /> <br /> Yet unbelievably complicated, even more so than the regular Pantheons that are by and large classified via geography and culture. You know of any cultures that <i>don't </i>have some nutty ass zeitgeist, told to children before they go to bed? A Pantheon based around Fairy Tales and Nursery Rhymes would be fuckin' enormous.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:orangered\">Duly Elected Guild Hierarch</span> of the Exalted Forum<br /> <a href=\"http://northernfoxgames.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://northernfoxgames.com/</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/331-the-revenge-of-tv-head","name":"The Revenge of TV Head","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar331_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1272"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-04T16:57:33-08:00","id":65235,"title":"","content":"What about clumping them in archetypes?<br /> <br /> Out of my mind: <br /> <br /> - The Gift - Bringer (Santa Claus, Saint Nicholas, the easter bunny, the tooth fairy,...)<br /> <br /> - The one which kidnaps in darkness (Krampus, the Babau, the Boogeyman, Sack man, etc\u2026)<br /> <br /> - The divine children (Child Jesus, (any book in which there is a &quot;special&quot; children), princes and princesses)<br /> <br /> - The funny guard (any &quot;strong&quot; imaginary friend, where wild things are\u2026)<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1304-baroqo","name":"Baroqo","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"67"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-07T07:58:55-08:00","id":67197,"title":"","content":"Check my post in the fan-written material for a incomplete version along the same lines that was on the old wiki","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2099-brothersale","name":"brothersale","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2099_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-10T02:17:25-08:00","id":68913,"title":"","content":"Interesting.<br /> <br /> Though there are so many fairy tales, told and re-told over time and across the world that the pantheon would labor under the most tangled fatebinding imaginable.<br /> <br /> As Baroqo mentioned, such a pantheon would probably be less about individual gods, and more like archetypes that wear whatever face is most popular at the time - probably more than one, since any individual gods that were born out of fairy tales probably got mashed together with similar beings long ago.<br /> <br /> So going by my limited knowledge of mythology...(and suggested purviews?)<br /> <br /> The king/leader/skyfather - Any kind of good king-figure, commonly a handsome prince. Questor, leader-of-men.<br /> Epic charisma, strength - justice, sun,<br /> <br /> Earth-mother/pantheon queen - Beautiful princesses abound. Always clever, likes riddles (in the old stories) spunky and willful (newer versions) and always good with animals.<br /> Epic appearance, dexterity, wits - animal, health, sky<br /> <br /> The bad guy - The boogey-man, the evil wizard. More sneaky than schemer, since convoluted plans aren't as terrifying as siccing monsters on the good guys.<br /> Epic intelligence, manipulation - illusion, darkness, death, chaos<br /> <br /> The craftsman - Either delivers or makes the gifts. Gepetto or Santa. Loves kids, but has no kids of his own. (Sterile, he creates toys to substitute for the children he can't have, adopts scions.)<br /> Epic intelligence, stamina - earth<br /> <br /> The mystic - The wise fairy godmother. Magic out the wazoo, but never gets directly involved, preferring to only supply the means to save the day.<br /> Epic socials - Magic, mystery, moon, water<br /> <br /> The mentor - Doddering old wise dude. Long, hard lessons on practical matters. Argues with the fairy godmother like an old married couple.<br /> Epic mentals - Magic, prophecy, stars, fire<br /> <br /> The warrior - Slayer-of-nightmares. big eater, big guy, big belly. gentle giant. <br /> Epic physicals - Guardian, war,<br /> <br /> The worker - fazed by nothing, sometimes a wanderer, simple living, good with his hands and plants. <br /> Very grab-bag Epics and perviews - Stamina, psycopomp, fertility, animal, earth<br /> <br /> <br /> ...okay, someone else please take over now.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1457-quanutmshard","name":"Quanutmshard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1457_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"61"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-10T19:43:56-08:00","id":69569,"title":"","content":"Okay we have a pretty serviceable pantheon of Gods (though I wouldn't say no to a Wicked Stepmother Goddess, however that would work).<br /> <br /> Now for the part I hate: Titans.<br /> What makes for a good Fairy Tail Titan?<br /> <br /> Only idea I have is The Beast: A Vicious feral thing, Big Bad Wolf (either version), The monster that hides under your bed or your closet. The Devourer of Worlds and Children.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:orangered\">Duly Elected Guild Hierarch</span> of the Exalted Forum<br /> <a href=\"http://northernfoxgames.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://northernfoxgames.com/</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/331-the-revenge-of-tv-head","name":"The Revenge of TV Head","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar331_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1272"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-11T05:36:42-08:00","id":69781,"title":"","content":"That which devours the hopes and dreams of children. Could be a lot of things. Capt. Hook; Wicked stepmother; Big Bad Wolf; Wicked Witch of the North; Ebenezer Scrooge (who becomes redeemed when he's forced to rediscover his own inner child). &quot;Age&quot; could be seen as a common theme, when it's allowed to beat a person down. When cynicism replaces hope; what is believed to be &quot;practical&quot; replaces dreams; when a person believes s/he's too old, that person has died inside.<br /> <br /> A &quot;Titan&quot; we can all hate.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-11T07:34:26-08:00","id":69837,"title":"","content":"Hm. Hunger seems a good concept for a Titan of fairytale, that which constantly wants, but is never satisfied, no matter how much it claims for itself. Despair seems a good concept for a Titan that _opposes_ fairytale, representing soul-death, dream-death, cynicism and the crushing of hope.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-12T01:19:10-08:00","id":70413,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>marin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/64147-the-children-s-pantheon-deities-based-on-nursery-rhymes-and-fairy-tales?p=69837#post69837\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hm. Hunger seems a good concept for a Titan of fairytale, that which constantly wants, but is never satisfied, no matter how much it claims for itself. Despair seems a good concept for a Titan that _opposes_ fairytale, representing soul-death, dream-death, cynicism and the crushing of hope.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm getting some serious Kingdom Hearts vibes here...<br /> <br /> Preta, the Titan of Emptiness.<br /> Favored Purviews: Illusion, Chaos, Psychopomp, Darkness<br /> Banned Purviews: Fertility, Guardian<br /> <br /> <br /> Preta represents emptiness in all it's forms, each of it's avatars opposed to or hungering for a specific thing - youth, hope, life, material goods...and what it cannot possess, it is driven to snuff out. The Titan's dimension-body takes the form of vast enclosed spaces, where it piles high what it has stolen in an unending and ever-futile attempt to be sated. Each of Preta's avatars shape an area of the Titan to its personal taste, which always take the form of an enclosed area.<br /> <br /> <u>Avatars:</u><br /> <br /> She is the Other Mother, the parent-figure who gives the child everything they want.<br /> She rules over a sprawling house where blank-eyed children go through the routines of play, kidnapped by a sterile Avatar who desired children of it's own. The Titans plays with and dotes each child at first, but her attention slowly drinks away their joy and sanity. Spent children are quickly forgotten in favor of fresher catches who still possess the capacity to feel joy and offer their love...<br /> <br /> He is the Spoiled Child, always wanting, never satisfied.<br /> He lives in a a rough-hewn stone cavern piled high with stolen toys, keepsakes, and even pets, taken for the emotions attached to them rather than any material worth. They only have value as long the item is remembered and yearned for, once forgotten or replaced, the item is vandalized and discarded, where scraps of affection among the refuse draw the broken items together into strange, composite golem-creatures.<br /> <br /> She is the Hag, old and weathered, bitter towards youth.<br /> Almost always appearing as a place of safety to lost children, though the sky is painted on the ceiling and the surrounding environment enchanted to befuddle minds and drive them to where the hag waits. She desires to experience youth once again, however fleeting the sensation, captured children rendered down to something her broken teeth can digest in a cauldron of black iron. Though blind and forgetful, everything in her realm, from the walls and furniture of her home to the plants and animals that surround it are all under her total control.<br /> <br /> He is the Orphan, looking for a loving parent to provide what he was denied.<br /> When he spies an ideal parental figure, he spirits them away to his realm. They awake to find a world that is the same that they had left, yet the illusion can never be absolutely perfect. Their lives become easy and pleasant, with far more free time to dote on what they believe to be their child, but it is inevitable that they realize that something is amiss, that the world is not right, and that child they have been caring for is not their own. When the illusion is shattered, the parents are abandoned to starve in a maze of distorted mirrors while the Orphan leaves to find a new parent. Should his home be invaded, he calls out to his past caretakers once more, and their remains come shambling to his aid, armed with shards of the same mirrors that watched their deaths, which still carry reflections of that painful on their surface and edge.<br /> <br /> EDIT: While such an antagonist might make good shock value for adult scions, what about some kind of &quot;Scion: Innocents&quot; game, with a fairytale pantheon that visits it's scions at a much younger age than usual?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1457,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1457-quanutmshard","user":"Quanutmshard","date":". 02-12-2014, 04:22 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1457-quanutmshard","name":"Quanutmshard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1457_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"61"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}